  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA departing cold front and surface low pressure will lead to an\noverall drying trend today. However, as a mid-level shortwave\ncrosses the Middle Ohio Valley and lifts toward the Mid-Atlantic\narea, some rain showers should impact mainly areas south and\nsoutheast of Pittsburgh with some light rain. Neither heavy rain\nnor severe weather will be a threat. Showers should depart the\nsoutheastern ridges by midday with the shortwave.\n\nOtherwise, low stratus and patches of fog will linger overnight\nand through sunrise thanks to low-level moisture beneath a \nsubsidence inversion. Conditions should improve once daytime\nmixing commences, and a mostly sunny sky is forecast by\nafternoon in most locations. Low temperatures this morning and\nhigh temperatures this afternoon should come in just below\nseasonable levels.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure builds in the wake of the departing low, with dry\nweather and a trend towards seasonable temperatures continuing\nthrough Wednesday night.\n\nRain chances return Thursday, as the next shortwave trough \ndrops into the Great Lakes. This will drive a frontal boundary \ntowards our region, and there are ensemble indications that the \nboundary may stall somewhere in the Ohio Valley area into the \nweekend. This will keep the potential for rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms in the forecast through at least Saturday. \nFlooding and severe weather threats appear to be on the lower \nside at this distance, but trends will need to be monitored.",
